bevy-horizon: merge every present's fences so the compositor waits, not the CPU

For mesa-switch (https://github.com/danfromtico/mesa-switch, by danfromtico and NaGaa95; MIT)
at 953fa75a, after 0001-0032. scripts/build_nvk.sh applies it; this text before the first
"diff --git" line is ignored by git apply.

The Switch present path CPU-waits for the GPU every frame. The profile of the Horizon client
(log8, cpu-profile8.txt) shows it: 17.9% of the main thread's samples sit in
svcWaitSynchronization < waitObjects < eventWait < nvFenceWait, and the chain that reaches it is
Queue::present -> wsi_common_queue_present -> vk_device_copy_semaphore_payloads ->
nvkmd_switch_sync_copy_payloads -> vk_sync_wait_many -> nvkmd_switch_sync_wait ->
nouveau_horizon_fence_wait -> nvFenceWait. The CPU and GPU take turns: the frame's rendering is
queued, then the CPU blocks until it is done before the buffer is handed over, so neither side
overlaps the other.

vk_device_copy_semaphore_payloads is the WSI's no-command-buffer shortcut: the semaphores the app
passed to vkQueuePresentKHR are the ones its submits signaled, so their native fences already say
when the GPU is done with the image, and the compositor (nwindowQueueBuffer takes an NvMultiFence)
can wait on those instead of the CPU doing it first. nvkmd_switch_sync_copy_payloads had that fast
path only when there was exactly one wait and it was a native fence. bevy_horizon records the
world and the HUD in separate submits, so wgpu-hal's get_present_wait_semaphores returns two
semaphores for the image, wait_count is 2, the fast path never fires, and every present falls back
to vk_sync_wait_many with an infinite timeout.

Merge instead: collect the fences of every wait into one NvMultiFence, skipping invalid and
duplicate fences, and import that into each signal. A point sync holds four fences and NvMultiFence
carries four, so the merge is exact while the total fits; when it does not, or a wait is not a
native fence (a timeline semaphore, or a submit that has not completed yet), fall back to the CPU
wait rather than drop a fence. The one-time _debug_printf lines say which way each present went,
so a hardware log shows whether the wait moved to the compositor or is still on the CPU.

This does not touch the WSI's own throttle wait (wsi_common.c waits on the previous present's
fence before reusing an image); that is a separate wait, and the diagnostic here is what tells the
two apart.

